.TH "WINDOW.SCREENX" "3JS" "September 2, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Window.screenX \- Window: screenX property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]screenX\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]Window\f[R] interface returns the horizontal distance, in CSS
pixels, from the left border of the user\(cqs browser window to the left
side of the screen.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] \f[CR]Window.screenLeft\f[R] is an alias of the older
\f[CR]screenX\f[R] property.
\f[CR]screenLeft\f[R] was originally supported only in IE but was
introduced everywhere due to popularity.
.RE
.SH VALUE
A number equal to the number of CSS pixels from the left edge of the
browser window to the left edge of the screen.
.SH EXAMPLES
In our \c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/dom-examples/screenleft-screentop/
screenleft\-screentop
.UE \c
\ example, you\(cqll see a canvas onto which has been drawn a circle.
In this example we are using
\f[CR]Window.screenLeft\f[R]/\f[CR]Window.screenTop\f[R] plus
\f[CR]Window.requestAnimationFrame()\f[R] to constantly redraw the
circle in the same physical position on the screen, even if the window
position is moved.
.PP
See \f[CR]Window.screenLeft\f[R] for more information.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]window.screenLeft\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]window.screenY\f[R]
